  This is the endpoint taxonomy.fetch_taxon_lineage_xml actually calls at
  runtime. Every other taxonomy fixture here was assembled from the NCBI
  Taxonomy BROWSER, whose rank vocabulary DIFFERS: the browser says
  "no rank" where eFetch says "clade" (Opisthokonta, Vertebrata, Theria,
  Eutheria, Boreoeutheria and others).

  That difference had never been exercised. The parser was written against a
  reconstructed wrapper and tested against browser vocabulary, while
  production consumes this. The two behave identically, because neither
  "clade" nor "no rank" appears in RANK_ORDER, so both are unranked. That
  was luck rather than design, and nothing pinned it until this file.

  A NOTE ON THIS COMMENT ITSELF
  The first version of this header used a double hyphen as an em dash, which
  is illegal inside an XML comment, and the fixture would not parse. It read
  as the parser failing on real NCBI data, which was alarming and wrong. The
  lesson kept rather than tidied away: when a check fails, establish whether
  the subject or the instrument broke before concluding anything.

  The real response also carries a DOCTYPE line, verified separately as
  handled by ElementTree; it is omitted here only because the entity
  reference in the URL above already exercises the same parser path.

  NCBI Taxonomy data is produced by the National Center for Biotechnology
  Information, U.S. National Library of Medicine, and is in the public
  domain. See NOTICE at the repository root.
-->
